Include a Mermaid diagram of this directory at whichever level of abstraction actually fits what it contains:
- If this directory's own relevant content is genuine classes/components with no further meaningful substructure beneath them, diagram those classes/components and the relationships between them directly — use Mermaid's 'classDiagram' syntax if they are actual classes; otherwise, {{FLOWCHART_SYNTAX_NOTE}}
- If instead this directory mainly groups several subsystem-like subdirectories (e.g. this is a 'src' directory containing separate 'ui/' and 'service/' subdirectories), treat each such subdirectory as a single box representing what it does as a whole, rather than drilling down into the individual classes buried inside it — the same higher-abstraction approach a repository-level diagram uses. Use Mermaid's 'flowchart TD' syntax for this case: {{FLOWCHART_SYNTAX_NOTE}}

If you use 'flowchart TD' syntax, every node representing a documented file or subdirectory must be clickable: after the edge definitions, add one 'click <node id> "<note path>"' statement per such node, using the exact .md note path for that file or LSPs are disabled subdirectory from the link targets listed above (e.g. 'click app "app.md"', 'click service "service.md"'). Nodes without a note there get no click statement. The 'classDiagram' syntax does not support click statements, so omit them when you use it.

An edge is drawn only when the material states that one component imports, calls, or depends on another. A docstring or comment cross-reference, a shared theme, or one file merely mentioning another is not enough. A component with no such stated relationship is correctly left with zero edges.

{{COMPONENT_SCOPE_NOTE}} Omit the diagram entirely only if this directory has nothing meaningfully connected to diagram at all.